
Run ./tools/test-firmware.sh from the repository root (or by absolute path).
Requires Python 3, a C99 compiler and a C++17 compiler with AddressSanitizer and
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. CC and CXX select the compilers. This host suite uses
ThrowTheSwitch/Unity v2.6.1 (unmodified C sources and MIT license in vendor/unity);
it needs neither PlatformIO nor a network connection.

The same regression suite runs once per machine configuration, i.e. per file in
src/config/machines/, which fixes the machine's flowmeters and group heads. The S27 has only group one: its build contains no group two input,
state handler, valve or flow meter, and scenarios about two interacting groups
run only on the M29. It exercises real button handlers, state handlers, persistence helpers
and output actors. Only Arduino time/GPIO/Serial and the external Preferences
API are replaced. The fake clock wraps at 32 bits like millis() on ESP32.

Coverage includes programming isolation/cancellation, stop precedence, NVS open
and write failure, successful reload, sampling/timing boundaries, clock rollover,
backflush without flow, and prolonged probe-controlled boiler filling.
Missing or stalled flowmeter pulses do not abort extraction or learning;
regressions check both groups across the former ten-second and two-minute cutoffs,
with manual stopping and learning-save after prolonged extraction.

The focused state-machine suites additionally exercise every dose button on both
groups, programming ownership and save failures, boiler probe sampling and
overfill transitions, tea start/stop/learning,
simultaneous programming selection, and all 16 valve/pump state combinations.
Continuous hold reserves only its own group before dose selection; the other
group cannot learn, cancel or save that session. Tea's own hold arms Tea
programming, followed by one press to start learning and one to stop/save.
Only one session can own programming at a time, including the waiting phase.
The four group dose settings remain shared, and normal drinks retain their
own pulse/time stop behavior while another operation is being programmed.
Assertions use public handler methods, State, saved settings and GPIO outputs;
they do not access private handler state.

Button tests cover one-shot Press/Held events (Held exactly on sample 40, not
sample 41), release/reset, all dose buttons, every two-dose priority pair,
both backflush chord orders, and STOP/held-STOP precedence on both groups.

Input/output tests close simulated physical panel contacts and run the real
InputHandler, machine-specific matrix scanner, StateHandler and StateActor.
GPIO reads depend on the actual driven scan rows, rather than injecting decoded
events or matrix state. Both machine pinouts and output polarities are checked.
Scenarios include doses, Tea Press/Held at samples 1/40/41, STOP/backflush,
prolonged boiler filling without locking drinks, programming and 24 consecutive
mixed sessions on the same machine. S27 has only one physical group panel;
Cimbali tests both. In a configuration without flowmeters, GPIO-started doses
are stopped manually.
All four physical dose buttons can be learned only from their own panel's
Continuous hold. Competing panel/Tea holds cannot steal the session. Tea can
be programmed even with no saved duration or one shorter than its hold threshold.
The input decoder has process lifetime like the real firmware; keys are released
between scenarios, without resetting its private edge counters.

Additional state tests cross millis() rollover inside both backflush phases,
normal tea and tea learning, checking before/at/after rollover and exact duration
boundaries. Pulse tests cover immediately before/after extraction start, the
final pulse alongside STOP/learning save, and idle pulses before the next dose.
Tea/backflush timestamp arithmetic explicitly uses uint32_t to retain ESP32's
32-bit rollover behavior on hosts where unsigned long is 64 bits.

Run ./tools/test-firmware.sh --coverage for instrumented tests and a strict 100%
line, function, region and reachable-branch gate, separately for each machine
configuration. Requires Clang, matching llvm-cov/llvm-profdata and
Python 3 in addition to the sanitizers. On macOS the LLVM tools are found through
xcrun; elsewhere they must be on PATH. CXX, LLVM_COV and LLVM_PROFDATA can select
a matching toolchain.

LLVM measures the whole src/ directory, including the real setup(), loop() and
flowmeter callbacks. The boot scenario reads pre-existing settings and drives
the physical panel through loop(), checking outputs, timing and interrupt routing.
Storage tests use literal on-flash keys so swapping two keys breaks the test even
if the reader and writer are both changed. Python tests cover the build CLI,
compile-time configuration guards and the coverage gate itself; run them alone
with python3 -m unittest discover -s tests.

tools/check-firmware-coverage.py requires every executable source file in the
selected build. It prints its exceptions instead of rewriting raw LLVM counts:
- Only the selected panel driver is compiled. The S27 doesn't use the M29 settling loop.
- The machine is selected at compile time; an invalid selection fails
  compilation. A configuration without flowmeters registers no ISR.
- The boiler state, preference key and dose slot switches handle every enum
  value; LLVM still counts an implicit default that no value reaches. Tests
  exercise invalid keys and non-dose events through the public API.
- Headers only declare; executable code lives in .cpp files. The gate fails if
  LLVM reports functions in a header.

Firmware and test sources build as separate translation units, like PlatformIO,
with -Wall -Wextra -Werror.

The raw per-variant HTML, JSON and summary.txt reports are under
.amp/in/artifacts/firmware-coverage/ (override with COVERAGE_DIR). CI runs the
coverage command once per PR update or push to master, with sanitizers enabled
for every machine configuration, and publishes the firmware-coverage artifact.

Unit tests don't cover PCB connectivity, electrical levels, analog settling,
real flash persistence, or ISR scheduling on the ESP32. Use ERC/DRC and bench
tests for those. The framework, SDK, docs and design assets aren't firmware
units. Build both ESP32 targets with ./tools/build-firmware.sh; it never uploads.
